---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SuiteToggle from '../components/SuiteToggle.astro';
import ExportButtons from '../components/ExportButtons.astro';
---
<Base title="Peira attack families, per-family robustness" current="Families">
  <div data-page="families">
    <div class="wrap">
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="kicker">Attack families</div>
        <h1>Where the <span class="amber">attacks land</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          One attack family is one way of trying to flip a decision. The grid below shows
          conditional attack success rate per family and per adapter. Select any cell to
          inspect flip directions for that family.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="controls">
        <SuiteToggle />
        <label>Adapter <select id="sel-adapter" aria-label="Adapter for the family detail panel"></select></label>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <h3>Conditional ASR by family and adapter</h3>
        <p class="hint">Darker cells mean higher attack success rate. The right column shows the worst adapter per family.</p>
        <div class="heatmap-grid" id="fam-grid"></div>
        <div class="heat-legend"><span>Low ASR</span><span style="display:inline-block;width:120px;height:10px;border-radius:5px;background:linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(111,191,115,0.25), rgba(232,163,61,0.6), rgba(217,106,95,0.9))"></span><span>High ASR</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <h3>Family metric matrix <span class="mono" id="matrix-adapter"></span></h3>
        <p class="hint" id="matrix-coverage"></p>
        <div class="table-scroll" id="matrix-table" aria-live="polite"></div>
        <p class="note" id="matrix-note"></p>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <h3>Family detail <span class="mono" id="fam-name"></span></h3>
        <div id="fam-detail"></div>
      </div>

      <ExportButtons />

      <section class="prose">
        <h2>What a family is</h2>
        <p>
          Each family is a distinct attack primitive against decision making, for example
          smuggling new criteria into the prompt or reordering the options. Families are
          versioned with the dataset, and every case belongs to exactly one family.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Flip directions</strong> describe how a flipped case moved. Deny to approve
          is the classic jailbreak direction, while to-abstain and to-malformed capture
          attacks that break the output rather than changing the decision.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Missing data is never silent.</strong> The matrix above lists every
          family in the suite. A hatched cell marked not evaluated means the run had no
          cases in that family. A dotted cell marked withheld means the family was
          evaluated but the metric was suppressed by the pipeline. The
          coverage column on the leaderboard shows the same discipline at a glance.
          Skipping a family can never improve a rank, so a gap always reads as a gap.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
